<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>

<!--
Cosmic muons surviving the 2450 m.w.e. rock overburden of the Canfranc Underground Laboratory (LSC).

The slant thickness of rock grows as h0/cos(theta), so the intensity per unit solid angle falls much
faster with the zenith angle than at sea level: I(theta) ~ cos^6(theta) instead of cos^2(theta). At 60 deg
the muon crosses twice as much rock and the intensity drops by about two orders of magnitude. The
azimuthal anisotropy of the Monte Tobazo affects the total rate by less than 5% and is neglected here.
Reference: Trzaska et al., Eur. Phys. J. C 79, 721 (2019).

The "LscMuons" formula is sin(theta)*cos^6(theta), where the sin(theta) is the jacobian of
dOmega = sin(theta) dtheta dphi, needed because the formula is sampled on theta. It must be used with the
"cosmic" generator, which samples the primary position on a disk perpendicular to the direction, so the
projected sampling area does not depend on the direction. If instead the primaries are launched from a
horizontal plane ("wall" or "circle" surface generator), use "LscMuonsWall" (sin*cos^7), which includes
the extra cos(theta) projection of the plane.

The energy is monoenergetic at 245 GeV, the mean muon energy at 2450 m.w.e. This is enough for direct
muon tracks: the muon is a MIP crossing the detector in a straight line and dE/dx varies by less than 1%
between 150 and 245 GeV on the Fermi plateau.
WARNING: this approximation is ONLY valid for the direct muon tracks. For muon induced neutrons the
spectrum does matter, since the neutron yield scales as E^0.75, and a monoenergetic approximation would
underestimate the production by a factor of about 20. Use the full spectrum in that case.

Only mu- is generated. The real charge ratio at the LSC is about 1.3, but the "cosmic" generator accepts a
single source, and the track of a mu+ is identical to the one of a mu- for this purpose.

The absolute rate is not given by Geant4. TRestGeant4Metadata::GetEquivalentSimulatedTime() knows about
this generator and returns the live time as N_generated / (J * A_sampling), with
J = Phi_horiz * (n + 2) / (n + 1) and Phi_horiz = 5e-7 cm-2 s-1 the reference flux through a horizontal
plane. The rate of muons crossing the detector is then N_crossing / live time.
-->

<restG4>
    <globals>
        <parameter name="mainDataPath" value=""/>
        <parameter name="verboseLevel" value="essential"/>
    </globals>

    <TRestRun name="LscMuons" title="LscMuons">
        <parameter name="experimentName" value="LscMuons"/>
        <parameter name="runType" value="simulation"/>
        <parameter name="runNumber" value="1"/>
        <parameter name="runTag" value="LscMuons"/>
        <parameter name="outputFileName" value="LscMuons.root"/>
        <parameter name="runDescription" value=""/>
        <parameter name="user" value="${USER}"/>
        <parameter name="overwrite" value="off"/>
        <parameter name="readOnly" value="false"/>
    </TRestRun>

    <TRestGeant4Metadata name="lscMuons" title="LscMuons">
        <parameter name="gdmlFile" value="geometry-lsc/setup.gdml"/>
        <parameter name="seed" value="171981"/>
        <parameter name="nEvents" value="20000"/>
        <parameter name="saveAllEvents" value="true"/>
        <parameter name="printProgress" value="true"/>
        <generator type="cosmic">
            <source particle="mu-">
                <angular type="formula" name="LscMuons" direction="(0,-1,0)"/>
                <energy type="mono" energy="245GeV"/>
            </source>
        </generator>

        <detector>
            <parameter name="energyRange" value="(0,10)" units="GeV"/>
            <volume name="gas" sensitive="true" maxStepSize="1mm"/>
        </detector>

    </TRestGeant4Metadata>

    <TRestGeant4PhysicsLists name="default" title="First physics list implementation">
        <parameter name="cutForMuon" value="1" units="mm"/>
        <physicsList name="G4EmStandardPhysics_option4"/>
        <physicsList name="G4DecayPhysics"/>
    </TRestGeant4PhysicsLists>
</restG4>
